    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d362">

                <pb facs="0500" n="488" xml:id="pg488"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/7121: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Fish</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-12-11T20:00:00+02:00">December 11,
                            1940—8 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 11—3:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>344. The British Ambassador told me this evening that General Wavell<anchor
                        n="22" xml:id="d362-f1"/> had just advised him that the British had captured
                    Sidi Barrani.</p>
                <p>The number of prisoners was at the time unknown but included three generals.</p>
                <p>General Wavell had further informed the Ambassador that the Italians were fleeing
                    and the British were in pursuit.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Fish</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d362-f1" n="22" xml:id="d362-f1-content">
                <p>Gen. Sir Archibald P. Wavell, Commanding General of British Forces in the Middle
                    East.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
